#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2009|month=December|last=Gilbertson|first=Al|last2=Gudlewski|first2=Benjamin|last3=Johnson|first3=Mary|last4=Maltezos|first4=George|last5=Scherer|first5=Axel|last6=Shigley|first6=James|title=Cutting Diffraction Gratings to Improve Dispersion (“Fire”) in Diamonds|journal=Gems &amp; Gemology|volume=45|number=4|page=260–270|publisher=Gemological Institute of America|url=https://www.gia.edu/gems-gemology/winter-2009-fiery-diamonds-gilbertson|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240730045945/https://www.gia.edu/gems-gemology/winter-2009-fiery-diamonds-gilbertson|archivedate=30 July 2024|doi=10.5741/gems.45.4.260|text=In other words, the more times a light ray reflects within a diamond, the greater the separation of the spectral colors—and the more obvious the appearance of '''fire'''—will be. '''Fire''' in a gemstone is best defined as "the visible extent of light dispersed into spectral colors" (Reinitz et al., 2001). In a polished diamond, this is seen as flares or flashes of color that appear and disappear as the diamond, the observer, or the light source moves.|accessdate=20 February 2025}}

