===Etymology 1===
Origin {{unc|en|nocap=1}}; but probably of {{der|en|gmq}} origin. Probably either a back-formation from {{m|en|foggy||covered with tall grass; thick, marshy}}, from the earlier-attested {{m|en|fog||tall grass}} (see below),<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|fog}}</ref><ref name="Etymonline">{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> or from or related to {{bor|en|da|fog|t=spray, shower, drift, storm}},<ref name="Etymonline"/> related to {{cog|is|fok|t=spray, any light thing tossed by the wind, snowdrift}}, {{cog|is|fjúka|t=to blow, drive}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*feukaną|t=to whisk, blow}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pug-|t=billow, bulge, drift}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*pew-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*pow-|t=to blow, drift, billow}}, in which case related to {{cog|de|fauchen|t=to hiss, spit, spray}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q37477}}{{lb|en|uncountable}} A thick [[cloud]] that forms near the ground; the [[obscurity]] of such a cloud.
#: {{syn|en|haze|mist}}
#: {{coi|en|a bank of '''fog'''}}
#* {{RQ:Belloc Lowndes Lodger|I|0016|passage=Thus the red damask curtains which now shut out the '''fog'''-laden, drizzling atmosphere of the Marylebone Road, had cost a mere song, and yet they might have been warranted to last another thirty years. A great bargain also had been the excellent Axminster carpet which covered the floor;{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=Everybody Comes to Rick’s: “Casablanca” on the Big Screen|author=David Denby|date=March 19, 2012|url=https://www.newyorker.com/culture/culture-desk/everybody-comes-to-ricks-casablanca-on-the-big-screen|work=The New Yorker|passage=Wallis and Curtiz eventually agreed to shoot Howard Koch’s preferred ending, with distraught Ilse{{sic}}, still in love with Rick, going off with Laszlo to America, and Rick and Louis going off together into the '''fog'''. (In Morocco? '''''Fog'''''? Never mind.)|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260910150409/https://www.newyorker.com/culture/culture-desk/everybody-comes-to-ricks-casablanca-on-the-big-screen|archivedate=10 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[mist]] or [[film]] [[clouding]] a surface.
#: {{syn|en|steam}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A state of [[mind]] characterized by [[lethargy]] and [[confusion]].
#: {{syn|en|daze|haze}}
#: {{ux|en|He did so many drugs, he was still in a '''fog''' three months after going through detox.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=4|passage=I was on my way to the door, but all at once, through the '''fog''' in my head, I began to sight one reef that I hadn't paid any attention to afore.}}
# {{lb|en|photography}} A [[silver]] [[deposit]] or other [[blur]] on a [[negative]] or developed photographic image.
# {{lb|en|computer graphics}} [[distance fog|Distance fog]].

=====Usage notes=====
* To count the sense '''thick cloud''', "[[bank]] of fog" is usually used.
* To count the sense '''clouding a surface''', "[[foggy]] [[patch]]" is usually used.

