#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas Talfourd|Thomas [Noon] Talfourd]]| title=Report of the Proceedings Connected with the Grant Soirée of the Manchester Athenæum, Held on Thursday, October 23rd, 1845. From the Manchester Guardian of Saturday, October 25th, 1845. Printed for the Directors| location=Manchester|publisher=Cave and Sever, Printers, 18, St. Ann's Street| year=1845| page=16| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IKZfAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA16| oclc=263688495| passage=For although party's worn-out moulds have been shivered, and names which have '''flashed''' and thundered as the watchwords of unnumbered struggles for power are now fast waning into history, it is too much to hope, perhaps to desire, until the education of mankind shall more nearly approach its completion, that strong differences of opinion and feeling should cease to agitate the scenes on which freemen are called to discharge political duties.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Alfred Tennyson]]|chapter=[[w:The Princess (Tennyson poem)|The Princess: A Medley]]| title=Poems by Alfred Tennyson. In Two Volumes| edition=new| location=Boston, Mass.| publisher=[[w:Ticknor and Fields|Ticknor, Reed, and Fields]]| year=1851| volume=II| page=163| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5HoRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA163| passage=But while he jested thus,&nbsp;/ A thought '''flashed''' through me, which I clothed in act.&nbsp;/ Remembering how we three presented Maid,&nbsp;/ Or Nymph, or Goddess, at high tide of feast,&nbsp;/ In masque or pageant at my father's court.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Matthew Arnold| chapter=Preface| title=Poems| edition=new and complete| location=Boston, Mass.| publisher=w:Ticknor and Fields| year=1856| page=20| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OmkgAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA20|oclc=2859714| passage=The Isabella [''{{w|Isabella, or the Pot of Basil}}''], then, is a perfect treasure-house of graceful and felicitous words and images: almost in every stanza there occurs one of those vivid and picturesque turns of expression, by which the object is made to '''flash''' upon the eye of the mind, and which thrill the reader with a sudden delight.}}
# To [[flaunt]]; to [[display]] in a [[showy]] manner.
#: {{ux|en|He '''flashed''' a wad of hundred-dollar bills.}}
