===Etymology 2===
{{ety|en|id=pool of water|:inh|enm:flashe}}
From {{inh|en|enm|flashe}}, {{m|enm|flaske}}, also found as {{m|enm|flosche}} and {{m|enm|flushe}} (whence modern English {{m|en|flosh}} and {{m|en|flush}}), used in Middle and modern English for bodies of water with varying emphasis on them being "pools" or "marshes".<ref>For example, in the 14th century ''Northern Verse Psalter'' (''Surtees Psalter''), Vsp D.7, the ''Yorkshire Writers'' edition by C. Horstmann (vol. 2, 1896), ''flaske'' (in the Hrl 1770 edition, ''flosshe'') glosses {{cog|la|lacus|lacum|lake}}.</ref> It is not entirely clear whether these constitute a single term with varied spellings, or have distinct etymologies. The form {{m|en||flash}}, {{m|enm|flashe}} is often suggested to be from {{cog|fro|flache}}, {{cog|fr|flaque}}, which is of {{cog|gem}} origin, akin to {{cog|dum|vlacke||an estuary, flats with stagnant pools}}. See {{m|en|flush}} for more on that form.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[pool]] of water, in some areas especially one that is [[marshy]], and/or one formed by [[subsidence]] of the ground due to mining. {{q|Compare {{m|en|flush||marsh; pool}}.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1646|author=w:Jeremiah Burroughs|title=The Excellency of Holy Courage in Evil Times
|passage=their hearts lie lumpish as a Log that lies in a '''flash''' of water seven years together}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1895|journal=The Naturalist|section='', page 232
|passage=The {{...}} woods, commons, ponds, {{'}}'''flashes''',{{'}} bogs, 'damp spots,' and ditches are, when the number and rarity of some of the species are taken into account, the richest botanical ground in Lincolnshire;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author=R. E. Bevan|title=Notes on the Science and Practice of the Controlled Tipping ...|page=40
|passage=It includes deep holes, wet and dry, and the hill-and-dale formations left by the mechanical excavation of sand, gravel, clay, ironstone and other surface-worked minerals; the heaps of shale, waste products and fuel ash resulting from deep mining, manufacturing and the generation of electric power; the '''flashes''', swamps and hollows caused by mining subsidence; the hill-side scars made by chalk, limestone and hardrock quarrying; and the ruins of abandoned industrial buildings.<ref>For interpretation of the meaning of this cite, cf. the ''English Dialect Dictionary''{{'}}s entry "flash", "A pool formed by the subsidence of salt-mines, Chambers' ''Jrn.'' (Dec. 1888); From settlements of the land caused by salt-works ‘in some places large lakes, called flashes, are formed, some of them more than 200 acres in area and gradually extending,’ ''Dublin Review'' (Apr. 1889) 431, in ''Lin. N. & Q.'' (Oct. 1891) 249</ref>}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1972|journal=The Naturalist|issue=920-935|page=127
|passage=In breeding season the important [...] sites are distributed along the Dearne and Don on the '''flashes''', marshes, ponds and oxbows, in the carrs around Doncaster, and the flooded gravel pits at Blaxton, Finningley, Sutton and Lound (see fig. 3).}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1986|title=A Directory of Neotropical Wetlands|page=544
|passage=A saline coastal lagoon, up to 3m deep, behind a sand bar, with brackish marshes ('''flashes''') and mangrove swamps. The lagoon has now been permanently opened to the sea.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Tim Dee|title=Four Fields|page=47
|passage=Spring waders passed through on migration, joining the fen to other '''flashes''', marshes, rivers, seas and oceans, flying from Africa to Siberia and landing en route only where their feet might be covered with water.}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} A [[reservoir]] and [[sluiceway]] beside a [[navigable]] [[stream]], just above a [[shoal]], so that the stream may pour in water as boats pass, and thus bear them over the shoal.

