# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[thrive]] or [[grow]] well.
#: {{ux|en|The barley '''flourished''' in the warm weather.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|page=1|chapter=1|passage='Twas early June, the new grass was '''flourishing''' everywheres, the posies in the yard—peonies and such—in full bloom, the sun was shining, and the water of the bay was blue, with light green streaks where the shoal showed.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[prosper]] or [[fare]] well.
#: {{ux|en|The town '''flourished''' with the coming of the railway.}}
#: {{ux|en|The cooperation '''flourished''' as the customers rushed in the business.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1795|author=Robert Nelson|title=A Companion for the Festivals and Fasts of the Church of England
|passage=Bad men as frequently prosper and '''flourish''', and that by the means of their wickedness.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Anthony Wood|Anthony à Wood]]|title=The History and Antiquities of the University of Oxford: In Two Books|url=http://books.google.com.sg/books?id=eOxEAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA661| volume=1| location=Oxford| publisher=w:John Gutch| year=1792| page=661| oclc=642441055| passage=One hall called Civil Law Hall or School, '''flouriſhed''' about this time (though in its buildings decayed) by the care of the learned and judicious Dr. [[w:William Warham|Will. Warham]] Principal or Moderator thereof {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c4glkm5g5l3t#Report|title=Manchester United 2-0 Manchester City|work=BBC|author=Simon Stone, Shamoon Hafez|date=17-1-2026|passage=Kobbie Mainoo, so often overlooked by Amorim, '''flourished''' alongside Casemiro in midfield as United controlled the game.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be in a [[period]] of greatest [[influence]].
#: {{ux|en|His writing '''flourished''' before the war.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To develop; to make thrive; to expand.
#* {{RQ:Bacon War with Spain|page=4|passage=But all that I ſhall ſay in this vvhole Argument, vvill be but like Bottomes of Thred, cloſe vvound vp, vvhich vvith a good Needle (perhaps) may be '''flouriſhed''' into large VVorkes.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[bold]], [[sweeping]] [[movement]]s with.
#: {{ux|en|They '''flourished''' the banner as they stormed the palace.}}
#: {{ux|en|The squirrel '''flourished''' its fluffy tail about as an alarm signal after its narrow escape from the cat.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make bold and sweeping, fanciful, or wanton movements, by way of ornament, parade, bravado, etc.; to play with fantastic and irregular motion.
#* {{RQ:Pope Dunciad|passage=Impetuous spread the stream, and smoking '''flourished''' o'er his head.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To use florid language; to indulge in rhetorical figures and lofty expressions.
#* {{RQ:Watts Logick|text=They dilate {{...}} and '''flourish''' long upon little incidents.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make ornamental strokes with the pen; to write graceful, decorative figures.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To adorn with beautiful [[figure]]s or [[rhetoric]]; to ornament with anything showy; to embellish.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1716|author=w:Elijah Fenton|title=an ode to John Gower
|passage=With shadowy verdure '''flourish'd''' high,<br>A sudden youth the groves enjoy.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|IV|1|text=To bring you thus together, 'tis no sin, Sith that the justice of your title to him Doth '''flourish''' the deceit.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To execute an irregular or fanciful strain of music, by way of ornament or prelude.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=Why do the emperor's trumpets '''flourish''' thus?}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To boast; to vaunt; to brag.
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To brandish (a weapon).
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be known to have been alive at a particular time or in a particular period, where one's birth and death dates are not known; to have been active during a specified period; [[floruit]]. 
#: {{ux|en|John Smith ('''flourished''' 1425-1432) was...}}

