#* {{quote-book|en|title=Everie VVoman in Her Humor|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:Edward Allde|E[dward] A[llde]]] for Thomas Archer,{{nb...|and are to be solde at his shop in the Popes-head-Pallace, neere the Royall Exchange.}}|year=1609|oclc=55193753|newversion=reprinted as|editor2=John S. Farmer|title2=Every Woman in Her Humor|url2=https://books.google.com/books?id=yOsVAQAAMAAJ&pg=PP132|series2=The Tudor Facsimile Texts|location2=[Amersham, Buckinghamshire]|publisher2=[John S. Farmer]{{nb...|Issued for subscribers by the editor of the Tudor Facsimile Texts}}|year2=1913|oclc2=974756059|passage=Now to ye all, be '''firmaments''' to ſtars, / Be ſtars to '''Firmaments''', and as you are / Splendent, ſo be fixed, not wandring, nor / Irregular, both keeping courſe together, [...]}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|1|6|8|url=https://archive.org/stream/Bible1611/Binder1#page/n78/mode/1up|passage=And God ſaid, Let there be a '''firmament''' in the midſt of the waters: and let it diuide the waters from the waters. And God made the '''firmament'''; and diuided the waters, which ''were'' under the '''firmament''', from the waters, which ''were'' aboue the '''firmament''': and it was ſo. And God called the '''firmament''', Heauen: and the euening and the morning were the ſecond day.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=II|lines=174–175|passage=[W]hat if all / Her ſtores were op'n'd, and this '''Firmament''' / Of Hell ſhould ſpout her Cataracts of Fire, [...]|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|reprint=1875|page=11|passage=There are Crowns of Glory to be given us; and Garments that will make us ſhine like the Sun in the '''Firmament''' of Heaven.}}
#* {{RQ:Young Night-Thoughts|page=357|passage=On Nature's ''Alps'' I ſtand, / And ſee a Thouſand '''Firmaments''' beneath!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=H. G. B.|title=The Bride of Parma|magazine=[[w:The Scots Magazine|The Edinburgh Magazine, and Literary Miscellany;{{nb...|a New Series of the Scots Magazine.}}]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:Constable & Robinson|Archibald Constable and Company]]|month=January|year=1825|volume=XVI|page=68|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fFs3AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA68|column=2|oclc=221608863|passage=And far above the gentle moon sails on / Through the blue '''firmament'''. It is a scene / That gives that spot of earth the air of Heav'n!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:James Bell (geographical writer)|James Bell]]|chapter=[Chinese Empire.] Chapter IV.|title=A System of Geography, Popular and Scientific,{{nb...|or, A Physical, Political, and Statistical Account of the World and Its Various Divisions.}}|location=Glasgow|publisher=[[w:Archibald Fullarton|Archibald Fullarton and Co.]] and {{w|Blackie and Son}};{{nb...|W. Curry, Jun., and Co., Dublin; and Simpkin & Marshall, London.}}|year=1831|volume=V|page=34|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=SFQBAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA34|oclc=1015512136|passage=Some of his &#91;{{w|Confucius}}'s&#93; philosophical principles are, [...] that the cause or principle of things must have had a co-existence with the things themselves; [...] and that the central point of influence, from which this cause chiefly acts, is the blue '''firmament''' (tien), whence its emanations are spread over the universe; [...] the sun, moon, stars, and elements, are considered also as composing the '''firmament''', or Teen, as the immediate agents of the Deity, and as the productive powers in creation.}}
#* {{RQ:Eliot Middlemarch|volume=II|book=III|chapter=XXIV|page=46|passage=But there was no spirit of denial in Caleb, and the world seemed so wondrous to him that he was ready to accept any number of systems, like any number of '''firmaments''', if they did not obviously interfere with the best land-drainage, solid building, correct measuring, and judicious boring (for coal).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:J. Robert King|chapter=Who are We?|editor=w:Brian Thomsen|title=Lancelot du Lethe|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Tor Books|month=December|year=2001|page=229|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=D8OtBbsSrPMC&pg=PA229|isbn=978-0-7653-0118-5|passage=On its far side, on the very horizon of the world, stood purple mountains, so tall and sharp that they bridged the '''firmaments'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Barbara Kingsolver|chapter=Small Wonder|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bTTt0YqXKokC&pg=PT34|title=Small Wonder: Essays|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:HarperCollins|year=2002|year_published=2009|isbn=978-0-06-186864-1|passage=The feeling I dread the most is not fear but despair—the dim, oppressive sense that the more things change, the more they stay the same; that each of us with a frozen heart "like an old-stone savage armed" will continue to move in darkness, lifting boulders, patrolling the '''firmaments''' of divisive anger.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alexander Miller|chapter=The Development of Theories of Meaning: From [[w:Gottlob Frege|Frege]] to [[w:John McDowell|McDowell]] and Beyond|editor=Michael Beaney|title=The Oxford Handbook of the History of Analytic Philosophy|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2013|section=part II (The Development of Analytic Philosophy)|page=658|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eMZoAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA658|isbn=978-0-19-923884-2|passage=[...] 'The Morning Star' and 'The Evening Star' have the same celestial object as ''Bedeutung'', but present that object in different ways, perhaps in one case as the object that appears in such and such a place in the morning '''firmament''' and in the other as the object that appears in such and such a place in the evening '''firmament'''.}}
