#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael Schaller|chapter=Japan: From Enemy to Ally, 1945–50|title=Altered States: The United States and Japan since the Occupation|location=New York, N.Y.; Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=1997|page=7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=D1KFROHI6cUC&pg=PA7|isbn=978-0-19-506916-7|passage=Europe dominated foreign policy concerns, followed by the Near East and China, where General [[w:George Marshall|George C[atlett] Marshall]] tried, in vain, to mediate a civil war. Japan glowed dimly in the foreign policy '''firmament'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Shannon Wiley|chapter=Introduction|title=Seaford, Delaware|series=Images of America|location=Charleston, S.C.|publisher=w:Arcadia Publishing|year=1999|year_published=2001|page=8|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Vprkhhow1OEC&pg=PA8|isbn=978-0-7385-0207-6|passage=By this time, Seaford was a town of 2,000 people, and, in the next decade, the poultry industry became a rising star in the '''firmament''' of economic growth as new housing and feeding techniques were introduced.}}
